  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger into early next \nweek.\n\nAs a nor'easter churns offshore of the Eastern Seaboard, steady west-\nnorthwesterly flow will continue across the Lowcountry and southeast \nGeorgia. Even though this will create a less favorable environment \nfor coastal flooding, the astro tide will still be elevated given \nthe recent full moon. Thus, still expect to see minor coastal \nflooding with each high tide cycle through early next week (esp. for \nthe Charleston Harbor).\n\nCharleston Harbor: With the ongoing environmental conditions, tidal \nlevels could reach 7.1 ft MLLW in the Charleston Harbor during the \nnext two high tides at roughly 9 pm this evening and 9 am Monday \nmorning. A new coastal flood advisory has been issued for the \nupcoming high tide. Thereafter, it's likely to see minor coastal \nflooding continue through early next week for the Charleston Harbor.\n\nFort Pulaski: While tides will remain elevated at Ft Pulaski, they \nshould remain below minor flooding ranging around 9 to 9.1 FT MLLW \nthrough the early part of this week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rising temperatures through Tuesday, remaining above \nnormal next week. \n\nAs a nor'easter continues to impact the northeast coast, pleasantly \nwarm conditions are occurring across the southeast region as a \nweak coastal trough sets up. Highs this afternoon are expected \nto peak in the lower to upper 80s, warmest south of the I-16 \ncorridor, with mostly clear skies. Did have to lower dew points \na smidge this afternoon through Monday, and lowered overnight \ntemperatures given a similar radiational cooling setup, with \nlower to upper 50s expected inland and lower/mid 60s along the \ncoast. \n\nTemperatures will continue to rise to start off the week as upper \nlevel ridging strengthens, with the coastal trough pushing further \ninland and up the coast each day as a surface high pressure moves \ninto the Ohio River Valley. As a result, Monday will be a touch \nwarmer as the mid-80s build northwards and upper 80s become likely \nsouth of the I-16 corridor, with widespread mid 80s to lower 90s on \nTuesday. Higher PWATs build into the region Thursday into Friday as \na subtropical high pressure develops off to our north, though \nprecipitation chances remain low due to the ridge axis sliding \noverhead during that period. Better chances for rain come as the \nridge axis slides eastwards later this week into the weekend.",
